  "notes": "Icelandic (íslenska). Most conservative modern North Germanic language. PRESERVED: 4-case system; 3 grammatical genders; subjunctive; thorn þ [θ] and eth ð [ð]; full umlaut (y, ø, œ). UNIQUE: Voiceless sonorants — hl [l̥], hn [n̥], hr [r̥]. QUANTITY: the stressed vowel is long in an open syllable and before a single short consonant, short before anything heavier — a geminate, a preaspirated stop, or a cluster (Flego & Berkson 2019, Table 3: mala [ˈmaːla] vs manna [ˈmanːa], skata [ˈskaːta] vs skatta [ˈskahta]; they state that preaspirated stops behave like geminates for the contrast). Length is therefore positional, not lexical: ⟨í ý ú⟩ are the qualities [i i u] and take their length from nucleus_stressed like every other vowel letter. GEMINATES: Icelandic keeps consonant length, unlike Danish — doubled ⟨bb dd gg ff mm nn rr ss⟩ are the long consonants [pː tː kː fː mː nː rː sː] (Flego & Berkson 2019, Table 3). PREASPIRATION: ⟨pp tt kk⟩ = [ʰp ʰt ʰk] — preaspirated, NOT long, so these are the one doubled series that is not a geminate. ⟨u⟩ = [ʏ] (front rounded!); ⟨á⟩ = [au]; ⟨au⟩ = [øɪ]. No stød, no tonal accent. Source: Árnason (2011).",
